    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3). The change is complete. Here's what I did:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe app is Spring PetClinic REST; telephone handling lived in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` and the `Owner` entity's Bean Validation.\n\n**1. `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 replaced the old \"strip non-digits, require exactly 10 digits\" logic with an E.164 normalizer used both for storage and duplicate detection (the existing `findOwnerByTelephone(telephone)` now compares E.164 values since `telephone` is the normalized string):\n\n```java\nprivate static String toE164(String raw) {\n    if (raw == null) return null;\n    String cleaned = raw.replaceAll(\"[\\\\s()-]\", \"\");          // strip spaces, dashes, brackets\n    String digits;\n    if (cleaned.startsWith(\"+\")) {\n        digits = cleaned.substring(1);                        // keep '+' and country code\n    } else {\n        String national = cleaned.startsWith(\"0\") ? cleaned.substring(1) : cleaned; // drop single leading 0\n        digits = \"61\" + national;                             // assume +61\n    }\n    if (!digits.matches(\"\\\\d{8,15}\")) return null;            // 8\u201315 digits after '+', else reject (400)\n    return \"+\" + digits;\n}\n```\n\nReturning `null` \u2192 the controller responds `400 Bad Request`.\n\n**2. `model/Owner.java`** \u2014 the entity's `@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` would have rejected the E.164 form on persist, so I replaced them with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` and dropped the now-unused `Digits` import.\n\n**3. `openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `telephone` field description (and example) to document the new E.164 behavior. The DB column is `VARCHAR(20)`, which comfortably fits E.164's max 16 chars.\n\nVerification against the spec examples:\n- `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` \u2713\n- `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456` (explicit country code kept) \u2713\n- `12` \u2192 `+6112` (4 digits) \u2192 rejected with 400 \u2713",
